bwfm: two calls to TessronOS's functions, for the security of a join
and for the chip's events (docs/tessronos/09-network.md 9.6.2, 9.6.8).

Why: bwfm_connect writes the chip's "mfp" as 0 just before the join and
gives the chip's own supplicant nothing (the firmware's "sup_wpa",
"sae_password", the PMK), so a join can have neither the protection of
management frames (IEEE 802.11w) nor WPA3-Personal (SAE). The chip's
firmware (CYW43455 7.45.234) does both itself, as its capabilities say
(mfp, idsup, sae), when it is set up for them before the join. The
first call comes after bwfm_connect has written what it writes and
before the join, so that TessronOS's function may write the rest; when
it writes nothing the join is as before. The second gives every event
of the chip (the supplicant's state, a roam) to TessronOS before bwfm
takes it, for the manager and the records; bwfm's handling is not
changed.

No BSD has this: OpenBSD's bwfm writes "mfp" as 0 as well and has no
SAE. Linux's brcmfmac sets the chip up the same way (brcmf_cfg80211_connect:
"wpa_auth", "auth", "mfp", "sae_password" or BRCMF_C_SET_WSEC_PMK,
"sup_wpa", then the join).

Applied by tools/netbsd/build.sh to a copy of the file
(docs/tessronos/09-network.md 9.6); NetBSD's tree is not changed.

--- a/sys/dev/ic/bwfm.c
+++ b/sys/dev/ic/bwfm.c
@@ -115,6 +115,10 @@
